{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/15/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9473c6fb\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nRecolor the sparse foreground pixels in scan order along their longer spread, cycling red, teal, and grey.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Every foreground object is a single isolated 4-connected pixel.\n- The foreground pixel set has a rectangular bbox whose row or column extent defines the scan direction.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- If the foreground bbox is at least as tall as it is wide, order foreground pixels row-major; otherwise order them column-major.\n- Recolor the ordered pixels by repeating red, teal, grey from the first pixel onward.\n\nKey insight:\n- The original foreground colors are only markers; the output color depends on each marker's ordinal position in the dominant scan direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9ba4a9aa\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReturn the ring object reached by following the guide path from the checkerboard object.\n\nInput structure\n- The input has one dominant background color, one 3x3 checkerboard object,\n  several 3x3 ring objects, and sparse guide pixels on a two-cell lattice.\n\nRule\n- Find the checkerboard object and its unique adjacent guide pixel.\n- Follow guide pixels of that color through two-cell horizontal/vertical steps.\n- Output the unique 3x3 ring object adjacent to that connected guide path.\n\nKey insight\n- The checkerboard object is the source, and the color of its attached guide\n  pixel selects the path whose far endpoint points at the answer ring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5587a8d0\" lines=\"11-27\">\n<description>\nRedraw foreground objects as nested square rings ordered by object size.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a 4-connected monochromatic component.\n- Foreground object areas are distinct.\n\nRule:\n- Sort the foreground objects by decreasing pixel count.\n- Create a square output with side length 2 * number_of_objects - 1.\n- The largest object color fills the outer ring, each next object color fills\n  the next inner ring, and the smallest object color fills the center pixel.\n\nKey insight: The objects' original shapes do not get copied; their areas\ndetermine only the outside-to-inside order of the nested square pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"67e490f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes inside a large template frame using shape-matched outside sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. One large monochrome frame object (template).\n- Inside the frame: holes of various shapes (non-frame-color regions).\n- Outside the frame: small monochrome sprites scattered on bg.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the frame's bounding box; fill entirely with frame color.\n- For each hole, find an outside sprite whose shape matches (rotation/flip allowed).\n- Fill the hole with the matched sprite's color. If multiple sprites match, use the most frequent color.\n\nKey insight: Sprites act as shape-coded color keys — their shape identifies which hole they fill, their color determines the fill color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ff2825db\" lines=\"18-36\">\n<description>\nSelect the majority interior marker color, then redraw the framed board as that color's frame plus its bounding-box outline.\n\nInput structure:\n- The top row is a copied palette made from adjacent equal color pairs.\n- Below it is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame around a black interior.\n- Non-black, non-frame interior pixels are marker colors; one marker color has\n  the unique highest count.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the palette row unchanged.\n- Pick the uniquely most frequent interior marker color.\n- Clear the lower board to black, recolor its outer frame with the picked color,\n  and draw the picked color's interior bounding-box border.\n\nKey insight:\n- The noisy marker cloud is not preserved as pixels; only the majority marker\n  color and its spatial extent define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f5f939b\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nMark the missing center of each plus-shaped arrangement of four two-pixel bars with yellow.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The single foreground color forms isolated 4-connected objects, each a horizontal or vertical two-pixel bar.\n- Some bars form an implied plus sign: two horizontal bars and two vertical bars around an empty center, with a one-pixel gap between the center and each bar.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input unchanged except at implied plus-sign centers.\n- For every background pixel with foreground bars at offsets 2-3 cells left, right, above, and below, recolor that center yellow.\n- The one-pixel gaps immediately next to the center in the four cardinal directions must remain background.\n\nKey insight:\n- The yellow pixels are not extending a line; they identify the shared missing center where four separated two-pixel bars align as a plus sign.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cbebaa4b\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.\n- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.\n\nRule:\n- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.\n- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.\n- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.\n\nKey insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db7260a4\" lines=\"37-56\">\n<description>\nDrop the singleton marker into the matching red U-shaped container, or make a bottom-row puddle if no closed container is hit.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background, one singleton marker pixel on the top row,\n  and one other foreground color forming vertical bars and bottom segments.\n- A closed container is a U shape: two vertical side bars with a continuous\n  bottom segment between them and an empty rectangular interior above the bottom.\n\nRule\n- Remove the top marker from its original cell.\n- If exactly one closed U-shaped container has the marker column within its\n  horizontal footprint, fill that container's empty interior with the marker color.\n- If no closed container is under the marker column, fill the whole bottom row\n  with the marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker does not identify a color by value; it identifies a vertical drop\n  column, and only a structurally closed U shape can catch and contain the fill.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8cc533f\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nComplete every colored sprite by applying the union of all aligned partial sprite masks.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Each non-background color appears as a separated partial copy of one common sprite mask.\n- The copies share a top-left-aligned sprite window; some copies may be missing lower or right edge pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop each same-color mask to its bounding box.\n- Pad the crops to the maximum observed height and width, aligned at the top-left, and OR them into one complete template.\n- Fill that template at every sprite location using the sprite's own color.\n\nKey insight:\n- No single input sprite is complete, but their missing cells are complementary, so the aligned union reveals the full mask.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6a980be1\" lines=\"46-61\">\n<description>\nProject the framed periodic stripe pattern across the canvas and erase the unused frame cells.\n\nInput structure\n- A bg canvas with a one-cell-thick rectangular frame of one color.\n- Inside the frame are two parallel full-interior marker bars of another color.\n- A third color fills the region between the marker bars on periodically spaced rows or columns.\n\nRule\n- Infer the periodic row or column mask from the third-color stripes inside the frame.\n- Paint the frame color along those full rows or columns, extended to the grid boundary.\n- Extend the marker bars across the full canvas and repaint the source-color stripe cells between them.\n\nKey insight\n- The outer frame is not preserved as a border; it supplies the color for the periodic stripes selected by the inner source pattern.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\nKey insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\" lines=\"120-133\">\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"dce56571\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nStraighten the single foreground mask into a centered horizontal segment of the same color and length.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- All non-background pixels share one foreground color.\n- The number of foreground pixels fits in one centered row of the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Erase the original foreground mask.\n- Count its pixels, keep its color, and paint that many pixels as a horizontal segment.\n- Place the segment on the middle row with equal background margin on the left and right.\n\nKey insight:\n- The original foreground shape is irrelevant except for its color and area; the output is the area normalized into the grid's central horizontal line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
